    "created_at": "2026-06-13T08:35:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-06-21T22:01:52Z",
    "body": "Would it make more sense for this to be a command invoker so that the button can be styled? I can't imagine the controls needing more than a play/pause button. \n```html\n  <img id=\"myanimatedimage\" src=\"anim.avif\" >\n  <button commandfor=\"myanimatedimage\" command=\"toggle-playback\">▶️</button>\n```\n\nedit: apologies, just read the related thread. So if I have a tiny low-quality gif, the controls would include a button to make it full screen (because Safari does not support `controlslist`)? I'd say that for many cases that functionality is beyond unneeded. It adds visual clutter to the UI and if the user actually clicks that button it's a negative user experience. I don't want to see a pixelated dancing baby gif displayed in full screen, and providing that functionality is user-hostile.\n\nThe CSS [image animation spec](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-image-animation-1/#:~:text=An%20alternative%20solution,demand%20is%20confirmed.) says:\n\n> An alternative solution, instead or in addition to letting authors build their own UI on elements where the [:animated-image](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-image-animation-1/#selectordef-animated-image) pseudo class matches, would be to enable authors to instruct the user agent to provide the UI itself when appropriate.\n\n> For example, an additional controlled value for the [image-animation](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-image-animation-1/#propdef-image-animation) property could be defined. When this value would be specified, initially, the image animation would not run, as if [paused](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-image-animation-1/#valdef-image-animation-paused) has been specified. Additionally, if the underlying [content image](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-image-animation-1/#content-image) actually is an [animated](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-image-animation-1/#animated-image) one, the user agent would provide some UI to allow the user to play and pause the animation, and would also make the element focusable.\n\n> If necessary, this can be added to a later version of this specification. However, it is uncertain whether authors would broadly use this, as they frequently prefer to design UI controls specifically tailored to their site. This specification therefore chose to defer working out the complexity of such a solution until author demand is confirmed.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
